\chapter{Scoped JSON owners and views}
\label{ch:28}
\index{Scoped JSON owners and views}

The intrinsic JSON profile pairs a lexical owner from \OnxCodeTerm{json\_parse\_or\_die} with nested views rooted in that owner. A view can traverse an immutable subtree without acquiring independent ownership. A scope that returns early still has to release the owners it acquired. Passing the view outside the admitted lexical region, copying the owner, or capturing the view in a closure is rejected.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=2,lastline=13]{../../on1x/examples/memory.on1x}
The local \OnxCodeTerm{nested} owner is created only on one path. The return value is an integer derived from a view of \OnxCodeTerm{root}; it survives cleanup because it is independent of both owners. In a test, exercise the early return and the fallthrough return. Convert the integer result to a view result in a separate negative case to check the boundary. For recoverable format handling through standard-library bridges, see \cref{ch:53}. Those adapters have separate status and ownership conventions.

\section{Trace the document tree and the cleanup tree}
Sketch the parsed document as a root object with one member, outer.
That member is an object with one member, items; its value is an array
with two elements. Looking up the two keys makes two views along a
single path. It does not parse either subtree again. On the true path
the separate parse of an empty object introduces another root, but the
size operation still names the items array in the first document.
Hence the first print is two. On the false path there is no second
parse, and the return measures the root object's members, not all
descendants. The next print is one. In the final loop each iteration
parses an array of three and prints three before its own root leaves
scope.

There are two roots to destroy on the early path, not four. Destroying
one of the views as if it owned its subtree would invalidate part of
the first document while the root still owns it. Conversely, keeping
the empty object alive would not prolong the life of the items view:
it has the wrong root. As a separate experiment, change the second
lookup key to one that is absent. The runtime reports a missing member
and terminates; it does not manufacture an empty array whose size is
zero. A test of the normal cleanup path should therefore use an actual
empty array if zero is the expected answer.

